  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 944 AM MDT Wed Sep 16 2026\n\nWET WEDNESDAY + THURSDAY:\n\nAnother surge of sub-tropical moisture is forecast to enter the \nregion today and stick around for the next 24-48 hours, making for \nan even wetter environment than observed over the past few days. \nPersistent  flow will continue to dominant over eastern Utah \nand western Colorado. Additionally, PWATs are forecast to be \nupwards of 300% of normal in some places, particularly focused \non the southwestern corner of Colorado. At this magnitude, PWAT \nvalues are likely to break records.\n\nWith this much moisture, cloud cover will certainly be around for \nthe next few days, keeping the daytime highs on the cooler side. \nWhile on the topic of cloud cover, it is important to note that \ncloud cover has the ability to dictate storm mode. Increased morning \ncloud cover is indicative of more stratiform rain showers, \nwhereas less morning cloud cover can lead to an eventful \nafternoon with more intense thunderstorms. Both of these \noutcomes could be observed over the next 24-48 hours, or even a \nmix of both. While we often talk about stratiform rainfall being\nless threatening to flash flooding concerns due to weaker \nrainfall rates, that is not quite the case through tomorrow. For\none, the lower levels are significantly saturated from previous\ndays rainfall in most places. This will lead to efficient \nrainfall and minimal absorption by soils. We've already seen \nsome vulnerable locations produce flash flooding and debris \nflows with weaker rainfall rates, which is an indication that \nthere is little room left for the land to take on more water. On\nthe other hand, just because stratiform showers may dominate \ndoes not mean that embedded stronger cells cannot develop. There\nare upper level dynamic forcing parameters in place that could \nlead to strong updrafts and localized heavy rainfall. With all \nthat being said, the hail and wind threat will be on the \ndownturn as hydrologic threats take precedence. Refer to the \nhydrology section below for more detailed information about the \nthreat. \n\nFRIDAY + BEYOND:\n\nBy Friday, the moisture pool driving this wet pattern rapidly \ndiminishes. However, even with this decrease in moisture, we remain \nconfident that the majority of the region will still see above \nnormal PWATs through the weekend. With the low pressure beginning to \ntrack eastwards, storm activity should become less pronounced. \nBottom line is that storm chances will be around Friday, but more \nscattered in nature. By the end of the weekend, storm activity looks \nto be mostly confined to the higher terrain as convection will \nmainly be driven by orographics.",
